        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains March 20 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 2 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For March 20 in music history, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes March 20.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Night Fever" had the longest run, with eight weeks, and "Bridge over Troubled Water" is second with six.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week, and two of those had only that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} were in the final week of their runs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only March 20 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the March 20 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called March 20.</p>
        <p>This March 20 in music history page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains March 20 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>Two of the runs were broken up by another song, so "week of run" counts only the weeks that song held number one.</p>

        <h2 id="early">March 20 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Grammy Sweep, a Posthumous Number One and a Nashville Folk-Rock Classic</h2>
        <p>The first three Marches of the decade brought a duo's farewell ballad, a singer's final hit and a country-rock record from Nashville.</p>

        <h3>1970: "Bridge over Troubled Water" by Simon &amp; Garfunkel</h3>
        <p>Paul Simon wrote "Bridge over Troubled Water," and Art Garfunkel sang the lead vocal.</p>
        <p>At the 13th Annual Grammy Awards in 1971, it won Record of the Year and Song of the Year.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one on February 28, 1970, and stayed there for six weeks.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with six weeks from February 28 to April 4.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the third of six.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/bridge-over-troubled-water/">Bridge over Troubled Water</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Me and Bobby McGee" by Janis Joplin</h3>
        <p>Kris Kristofferson wrote "Me and Bobby McGee," and Fred Foster shares the writing credit.</p>
        <p>Janis Joplin recorded it nine days before her death in October 1970, for the album Pearl.</p>
        <p>The single came out on January 12, 1971.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it the second posthumously released number one single in US chart history.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, March 20 and March 27, 1971.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 11 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Heart of Gold" by Neil Young</h3>
        <p>Neil Young recorded "Heart of Gold" at Quadrafonic Sound Studios in Nashville, Tennessee, in February 1971.</p>
        <p>James Taylor and Linda Ronstadt sang backing vocals, and they were in town for an appearance on Johnny Cash's television program.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it Young's only US number one single.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of March 18, 1972.</p>
        <p>The song is number 17 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">March 20 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Song Inspired by a Concert, a Belgian Import and a California Rock Hit</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a soul ballad with a famous origin story, a pop hit with French roots and a rock song about the Mississippi.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Killing Me Softly with His Song" by Roberta Flack</h3>
        <p>Charles Fox composed "Killing Me Softly with His Song," and Norman Gimbel wrote the lyrics.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Lori Lieberman was inspired by a Don McLean performance in late 1971, and that Fox and Gimbel denied her a writing credit.</p>
        <p>Roberta Flack's version came out in January 1973.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it spent a total of five non-consecutive weeks at number one in February and March.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data lists the weeks of February 24, March 3, March 10, March 17 and March 31, 1973.</p>
        <p>The O'Jays' "Love Train" held the top spot in between, for the week of March 24.</p>
        <p>The chart week that contains March 20 was the fourth of the five.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Seasons in the Sun" by Terry Jacks</h3>
        <p>Wikipedia calls "Seasons in the Sun" an English-language adaptation of the 1961 Belgian song "Le Moribond" by Jacques Brel.</p>
        <p>Rod McKuen wrote the English lyrics in 1963.</p>
        <p>Terry Jacks's version reached number one on March 2, 1974, and held the top spot for three weeks, according to Wikipedia.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with three weeks from March 2 to March 16.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the third and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/seasons-in-the-sun/">Seasons in the Sun</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Black Water" by the Doobie Brothers</h3>
        <p>Patrick Simmons wrote "Black Water" and sings the lead vocal.</p>
        <p>It appears on the Doobie Brothers' 1974 album What Were Once Vices Are Now Habits.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it the first of the band's two number one singles, and says it topped the Hot 100 on the chart dated March 15, 1975.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of March 15, 1975.</p>
        <p>The song is number 16 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="late">March 20 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Prohibition, an Oscar, a Soundtrack Giant and a Survivor</h2>
        <p>The last four Marches brought a song that began as a Prohibition tribute, a movie ballad, the longest run of the ten and a B-side that took over.</p>

        <h3>1976: "December, 1963 (Oh, What a Night)" by the Four Seasons</h3>
        <p>Bob Gaudio and Judy Parker wrote "December, 1963 (Oh, What a Night)."</p>
        <p>The first version was set in 1933, was titled "December 5th, 1933" and celebrated the repeal of Prohibition.</p>
        <p>The band disliked those lyrics, so Parker wrote new ones.</p>
        <p>Drummer Gerry Polci sings lead on the verses, while Frankie Valli handles the bridge sections.</p>
        <p>It reached number one in the UK on February 21, 1976, and on the US Hot 100 on March 13, 1976, where it stayed three weeks.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with three weeks from March 13 to March 27.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the second of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "Evergreen (Love Theme from A Star Is Born)" by Barbra Streisand</h3>
        <p>Wikipedia says Barbra Streisand composed and performed the song, with lyrics by Paul Williams.</p>
        <p>It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song at the 49th Academy Awards.</p>
        <p>At the 20th Grammys it won Song of the Year, tying with "You Light Up My Life."</p>
        <p>It was Streisand's second Hot 100 number one, with three weeks on top.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows the weeks of March 5, March 12 and March 19, 1977.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the third and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Night Fever" by the Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>The Bee Gees began recording "Night Fever" in April 1977 at the Ch&acirc;teau d'H&eacute;rouville in France and finished in September at Criteria Studios in Miami.</p>
        <p>Robert Stigwood asked the group for a song called "Saturday Night" for the film, but they disliked the title and had already written "Night Fever."</p>
        <p>They convinced him to use it and to rename the film Saturday Night Fever.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the song debuted on the Hot 100 at number 76 and reached number one, where it stayed eight weeks.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with eight weeks from March 18 to May 6, 1978.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the first of eight.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/night-fever/">Night Fever</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1979: "I Will Survive" by Gloria Gaynor</h3>
        <p>Freddie Perren and Dino Fekaris wrote "I Will Survive."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was released as the B-side to "Substitute," and DJs and radio stations later played that side instead.</p>
        <p>The single spent three non-consecutive weeks at number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data lists the weeks of March 10, March 17 and April 7, 1979.</p>
        <p>The Bee Gees' <a href="/blog/songs/tragedy-bee-gees/">Tragedy</a> held the top spot in between, for the weeks of March 24 and March 31.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains March 20 was the second of the three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 6 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/i-will-survive/">I Will Survive</a> page.</p>

